Why does this mutex not prevent the race?
thread1: if (!ready) { data = 1; ready = true; }
thread2: if (ready) use(data);

Reasoning: there is no mutex here at all, and even with one you must protect both the flag and the data consistently. The classic bug is that ready=true can become visible before data=1 (compiler/CPU reordering) unless both accesses are ordered. Also, check-then-act is racy.

Fix with a lock:
``cpp
std::mutex m;
{
  std::lock_guard<std::mutex> g(m);
  data = 1; ready = true;
}
{
  std::lock_guard<std::mutex> g(m);
  if (ready) use(data);
}
``

Or use std::atomic<bool> with release/acquire:
``cpp
ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
if (ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) use(data);
``

Rule: publication of data requires a release store; consumption requires an acquire load on the same atomic.

Reasoning: regular files usually accept the whole buffer because the page cache absorbs it (barring ENOSPC, signals, or RLIMIT_FSIZE). Sockets have a bounded send buffer; a non-blocking or signal-interrupted write can accept partially. Partial writes are always legal for both.

Correct pattern:
``c
size_t off = 0;
while (off < n) {
  ssize_t w = write(fd, buf + off, n - off);
  if (w < 0) { if (errno == EINTR) continue; perror("write"); break; }
  off += (size_t)w;
}
``

For sockets, also consider SIGPIPE (use MSG_NOSIGNAL or ignore SIGPIPE) and EAGAIN for non-blocking descriptors.
